  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nCurrent regional radar highlights scattered showers across the \nTennessee Valley. Admittedly, these showers overachieved today with \nmost locations receiving a few hundredths to a tenth of an inch of \nrain. While it's not much, it's more impressive considering the weak \nforcing and low level dry air eating into QPF potential. Showers \nwill continue to dissipate as the forcing weakens and shifts \nnortheast overnight. Temperatures will fall into upper 40s and lower \n50s tonight with mostly cloudy skies. \n\nSurface high pressure shifts offshore the Mid-Atlantic on Monday \nwhile the 500mb pattern becomes increasingly amplified. Mid-level \nridging builds across the southeastern CONUS with low level warm air \nadvection ahead of the mid-week system. This will set the table for \na mild and dry start with highs in the mid 70s and lows in the low \nto mid 50s Monday and Tuesday. Wednesday and Thursday will be the \nfocal point of this forecast period as the mid/upper level trough \nover the central Plains moves east toward the Ohio and Tennessee \nriver valleys. The associated cold front will slowly trek east on \nWednesday with rain chances increasing primarily west of interstate \n75 Wednesday afternoon. The beneficial rain will be fueled by an \nimpressive surge of anomalous PWATs (+2 std) ahead of the cold \nfront. Grand ensemble probabilities of 0.50\"+ highlight medium \nconfidence (40 to 60 percent) across the area. Rain chances spread \nfurther east Wednesday night with rain exiting the area by Thursday \nevening. Rainfall amounts will likely range from a quarter inch to a \nhalf inch with locally higher amounts possible. Thunderstorm chances \nwill be low, but non-zero. MUCAPE values in forecast soundings are \nquite low so at most a few rumbles of thunder may be possible. The \nsevere risk is negligible. This system will also bring the potential \nfor mountain wave downslope winds. EPS and GEFS ensemble \nprobabilities support sub-advisory wind gusts (35-45 mph) along the \nmountains and foothills Tuesday night through Thursday afternoon. \n\nPost FROPA will see the return of drier, more seasonable airmass \nsettle in for next weekend. Slight chance pops linger Thursday & \nFriday given northwest cyclonic flow behind the low pressure system, \nbut most locations should remain dry. High pressure will begin to \nbuild in for the weekend and this should return drier weather and \nreturn more sunshine for the first full weekend of November. Highs \nwill be in the low to mid 60s with lows in the upper 30s to lower \n40s.",
